      "bottom_line": "NULL RESULT, with a clear physical cause. The proper-time field is SMOOTH. The direct v.grad(phi) regression on GPS satellite clocks moving at 3.9 km/s returns a null (R^2 = 0.093 against a 0.125 noise expectation; recovered gradient vector temporally incoherent at 0.028, sign-flip null z = -0.1), bounding |grad phi| <= 1.7e-8 m/m at orbital altitude -- ~100x below the ground bound. Evaluating each platform at its own altitude's gradient, no platform clears 3x the measured clock-rate floor (4.1e-4 m/s): aircraft reach 1.3x, a road vehicle 0.15x, and an orbiting platform 0.4x because the field is so much smoother up there. Position-from-the-clock is therefore NOT available in this data. The reason matters: smoothness is exactly what makes the network predict a station's clock so well, so the property that powers Steps 55/56/64 is the same one that forecloses positioning. The only remaining window is fine structure below ~150 km, which no current product can resolve -- IGS clock products have almost no pairs there and SPP-derived dense products carry a 4-7 m noise floor. A dense CORS cluster processed with precise ephemerides would close it."
